In patterns, `$...X` inside an array or list literal (e.g. `[1, $...REST]`) now binds the remaining elements, as it already did among call arguments, and `...` inside a list pattern (e.g. `[1, ..., 9]`) now stands for any number of elements instead of exactly one. In C# and Dart, taint analysis treats `a?.f = x` as a write to `a.f` that only happens when `a` is not null, and reads through `a?.f` stay field-sensitive.
